---
components: ["Kanban"]
description: Kanban boards sort cards into columns that people move between by dragging, with the keyboard, or from a menu, such as incidents by stage or releases by status.
---

## Basic

Pass the `columns` in reading order and the `items` as one flat array. Each item has an `id` and the `id` of its `column`, and the order of the array is the order within a column. A card shows its `title`, an optional `description`, `tags`, and an `assignee`.

Bind `items` to apply moves to your data. The board never edits your array or its cards: it assigns a new array, which reaches you only through `bind:items`. Without `bind:`, the board still shows the move, and your array stays as it was until you set it again.

The board fills the height it is given, and each column scrolls on its own. Without a height, it grows with its tallest column. Name it with `aria-label`.

<FileSource src="/framed/Kanban/KanbanBasic" />

## Moving cards

Every way of moving a card ends in one `move` event, so the three stay in step.

### Pointer and touch

Press a card and drag it. The card follows the pointer, a line shows where it will land, and the column under it is outlined. Near the edge of a column the column scrolls, and near the edge of the board the board scrolls. A drop outside every column cancels the move.

A mouse starts a drag after a few pixels of movement, so a click still works. On touch, the grip at the start of a card lifts it at once, and a press that rests on the rest of the card for a moment lifts it too. A quick swipe on a card scrolls the column instead.

Links, buttons, and form controls inside a card keep their own press and don't start a drag. Put the action that opens a card on one of them, such as the title as a link, and the rest of the card drags.

### Keyboard

The board is a single tab stop. Focus lands on the grip of one card, and the arrow keys move focus between cards: <DocKbd label="ArrowUp" /> and <DocKbd label="ArrowDown" /> within a column, <DocKbd label="ArrowLeft" /> and <DocKbd label="ArrowRight" /> across columns, and <DocKbd label="Home" /> and <DocKbd label="End" /> to the first and last card of the column. The inline arrows swap in right-to-left languages.

Press <DocKbd label="Space" /> or <DocKbd label="Enter" /> to pick a card up. The arrow keys then move it, <DocKbd label="Space" /> or <DocKbd label="Enter" /> drops it, and <DocKbd label="Escape" /> puts it back. Nothing changes in your data until the drop. Moving focus away also puts it back.

<DocKbd label="Shift" />+<DocKbd label="F10" /> or <DocKbd label="ContextMenu" /> opens the card's move menu, and <DocKbd label="Escape" /> closes it and returns focus to the card. The menu button stays out of the tab order, so the board keeps one tab stop. A pointer, voice control, or a screen reader's browse mode still reaches the button.

A live region announces each step: the pick-up and its position, each new place, and the drop or the cancel. The grip is described as a draggable card, and a hidden description tells screen reader users the keys.

### Move menu

Each card has a menu with "Move up", "Move down", and "Move to" each other column, so a card moves without dragging. It is the way to move a card with a switch device, with voice control, or with a screen reader that doesn't pass arrow keys. A card moved to another column goes to the bottom.

## Move event

The `move` event fires once per move, on drop, with the card's `id`, the column it left (`from`) and entered (`to`), its new position among the other cards of `to` (`index`, where `0` is the top), where it was (`fromIndex`), and how it moved (`source`: `"pointer"`, `"keyboard"`, or `"menu"`).

The event is cancelable. Call `preventDefault()` to refuse the move, or to hold it until a server call settles. Then apply it with `applyKanbanMove(items, event.detail)`, which returns a new array and leaves yours and its cards as they were. It runs on the server too.

<FileSource src="/framed/Kanban/KanbanMoveEvent" />

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Read `index` as the card's final position | For a card reordered inside its column, it counts the other cards, so it needs no adjusting for the card's old place. |
| Replace `items` with a new array | The board compares by identity, and a card changed in place isn't picked up. |
| Cancel the event, then set `items` after the save | The board stays the source of truth you control, with no card left in the wrong column on failure. |

## Work-in-progress limits

Set `limit` on a column to cap the cards it should hold. Its count reads "3 / 4". A column over its limit shows a warning icon and a red count, and says "over its limit" to a screen reader and in the announcement when a card is dropped into it.

`limitMode` decides what the limit does. The default `"warn"` lets cards in and marks the overflow. Set it to `"block"` to keep cards out of a full column: the column is outlined as refused while dragging, the arrow keys stop at it and say it is full, and its "Move to" item is disabled. Reordering inside a column is always allowed, so a column over its limit can still be sorted and emptied.

<FileSource src="/framed/Kanban/KanbanLimits" />

## Collapsible columns

Set `collapsible` to give each column a button that collapses it to a narrow strip with its title and count. Set `collapsible: false` on a column to keep it open. Bind `collapsedIds` to read or set the collapsed ids.

A collapsed column is still a place to drop a card with the pointer, and the card goes to its bottom. Keyboard moves skip it, and a collapsed column builds its cards only when it first opens.

<FileSource src="/framed/Kanban/KanbanCollapsible" />

## Custom cards

Fill the `card` slot to replace a card's content, with the `item`, its `column`, and its `index`. The `columnHeader` slot replaces a column's title and count, and `empty` replaces the text of an empty column. Set `itemToString` to name cards for assistive technology when they have no `title`.

<FileSource src="/framed/Kanban/KanbanCustomCard" />

## Disabled

Set `disabled` to `true` for a read-only board. Cards don't drag and have no move menu.

## Text

Every string a person sees or hears is a prop: `instructionsText`, the announcements (`pickedUpText`, `movedText`, `droppedText`, `cancelledText`, `fullText`, `overLimitText`), `moveToText`, `moveUpText`, `moveDownText`, `collapseText`, and `emptyText`. The announcement props are functions that receive the card's label, the column's title, and the position, so a translation can reorder the sentence.
